{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Furthermore</b>, some say that ten kings will yet stand over Judah before the exile, and this is the meaning of <b>ושבה והיתה לבער</b>, like ובערת הרע (Deuteronomy 21:21). Others say it is from the root לא תבערו אש (Exodus 35:3). And in truth, such was the number of the kings including Uzziah. And after him they said that a tenth will remain in the land. But according to grammatical analysis of the language, עשיריה is an epithet, not like עשירית (a tenth), and the proof is ישראל שלישיה (Isaiah 19:24). Thus it is confirmed that this is an allusion to kingship." "<b>כאלה וכאלון.</b> Two trees were in the gate of Shalakheth and they were strong. Those who say that שכת is a reversal of לשכה are incorrect. Likewise those who derive it from the word השליך, for the one who says כאלה does not know the grammar of the language." "And the meaning of <b>מצבת בם</b> is that they will yet stand and be standing forever. Do not wonder that he compared them to a tree, for so it is: כימי העץ (Isaiah 65:22)." "And the meaning of <b>זרע קדש מצבתה</b>: the stock that will stand in it afterward is holy seed, and this is an allusion to those who went up from the Babylonian exile."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra reads עשיריה as a construct adjective modifying a feminine noun (i.e., 'its tenth part'), not as the absolute form עשירית.", 1 "Ibn Ezra appears to use the word 'gate' (שער) loosely for the precinct or area associated with שכת; the superlinear 'שלכת' simply follows the vowelization he assumes."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:52:36.178371959Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}